// FINDING A — a tor with TWO arms, where only ONE arm re-issues the input's
// phantom state and the OTHER arm genuinely converts it to a DIFFERENT
// state, is excluded as a disposal candidate for the state ENTIRELY — even
// down the arm that legitimately converts it, and even when that arm is
// called EXPLICITLY in the source (no auto-discharge insertion needed).
//
// Root cause, read directly from the compiler source (not guessed):
// `src/phantom_semantic_checker.zig:296` and `src/auto_discharge_inserter.zig
// :3057` both define `eventReIssuesObligation`, and both loop over EVERY
// branch of the tor and return `true` (excluded) as soon as ANY branch
// re-issues the base state — there is no per-arm distinction, and no check
// for whether at least one OTHER arm genuinely converts it. `settle` below
// has exactly that shape: `ok` converts <!built> to <held!> (genuine), and
// `refused` re-issues <built!> (a self-loop) — so `eventReIssuesObligation`
// returns true for the whole tor, `settle` is dropped from the candidate
// list, and the checker later reports "No tor accepts <!built>" even though
// this file calls `settle(obj)` explicitly and takes its `ok` arm.
//
//   koruc A_partial_reissue_excludes_whole_tor.kz
//
// Literal output (2026-08-07, koruc built from /Users/larsde/src/koru HEAD
// at the time of this lift):
//
//   error[KORU030]: Resource 'obj' obligation <built!> was not discharged. No tor accepts <!built>.
//     --> A_partial_reissue_excludes_whole_tor.kz:97:0
//       |
//    97 | | ready obj |> settle(obj)
//       | ^
//
//   ❌ Compiler coordination error: Auto-discharge failed (multiple disposal options or no disposal event)
//
// `--check` passes both this file and its control — shape-checking does not
// walk the phantom-obligation flow at all, so this is invisible until the
// FULL pipeline runs. Verified identical failure (same line, same message)
// under all three of `koruc <file>`, `koruc build <file>`, `koruc run
// <file>`. `koruc --check <file>` never reaches it, on either file.
//
// `--auto-discharge=disable` hits the SAME bug through the checker's OWN
// separate copy of `eventReIssuesObligation`
// (`phantom_semantic_checker.zig:296`, vs the inserter's copy at
// `auto_discharge_inserter.zig:3057`) — the wording differs slightly
// (confirming two independent code paths, not one):
//
//   error[KORU030]: Resource 'obj' carries obligation <built!> was not discharged. No tor accepts <!built>.
//     --> A_partial_reissue_excludes_whole_tor.kz:97:0
//   ❌ Compiler coordination error: Phantom semantic validation failed
//
// Does NOT need a cross-module import to fire: this file is single-module,
// no `~import`, no `|zig` FFI beyond a plain heap allocation, and it still
// reproduces. My original finding (in unikraft/store's README) was pinned
// only against a cross-module unikraft lift; this file establishes the
// defect is NARROWER-SURFACE than that framing implied — it needs no
// cross-module machinery at all, just the one-tor, two-arm shape above.
//
// Flip to A_control_distinct_state.kz — the ONLY change is `refused`'s
// output state, `*Obj<built!>` -> `*Obj<stuck!>`, plus the matching consumer
// `abandon` — and it compiles clean through the full pipeline.
//
// NOT the same finding as `440_007_reissuing_event_is_not_a_discharger` or
// `330_118_conserving_tor_is_not_a_disposal_candidate` in
// /Users/larsde/src/koru/tests/regression, though it is the SAME predicate:
// both of those pin a tor that reissues on EVERY declared arm (wholly
// conserving — genuinely has no disposer, and "No tor accepts" is the
// CORRECT diagnosis there). `330_118`'s own comment states the intended
// rule: "A CONVERTING tor is a different thing and stays a legitimate
// candidate... Only same-obligation conservation is disqualifying." `settle`
// below is NOT wholly conserving — its `ok` arm converts — so by that
// stated rule it should stay a candidate. It does not, because
// `eventReIssuesObligation` checks "does ANY arm reissue", not "do ALL arms
// reissue" or "does at least one arm genuinely convert".

const std = @import("std");
const Obj = struct { v: i32 };

~pub tor mk { } -> *Obj<built!>
~proc mk|zig {
    const o = std.heap.page_allocator.create(Obj) catch unreachable;
    o.* = Obj{ .v = 1 };
    return o;
}

// The ONE line that differs from the control: `refused` mints <built!>
// again — the SAME single non-union state `settle` also takes as input.
~pub tor settle { obj: *Obj<!built> }
| ok *Obj<held!>
| refused { obj: *Obj<built!>, reason: string }
~proc settle|zig {
    return .{ .ok = obj };
}

~pub tor finish { obj: *Obj<!held> }
~proc finish|zig {
    _ = obj;
}

~mk()
| ready obj |> settle(obj)
    | ok held |> finish(obj: held)
    | refused _ |> _
